    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Loretto was founded in 1799 by Demetrius Augustine Gallitzin as an English‑speaking Catholic settlement west of the Allegheny Front.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loretto%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from Loreto, Italy, the site of a famous Catholic pilgrimage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loretto%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Saint Francis University, a private Catholic university, sits in Loretto’s southwestern quadrant and has been a community hub since 1859.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loretto%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Allegheny Portage Railroad National Historic Site, just outside Loretto, showcases 19th‑century engineering that once hauled cargo over the Allegheny Front.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://pennsylvaniainfo.blog/loretto-pennsylvania-hidden-gems"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Loretto Heritage Center preserves over two centuries of papers and artifacts, offering a window into the borough’s history.",
